1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,525
Every page of an Orchard Core site is built from shapes.

2
00:00:04,525 --> 00:00:09,984
The Templates module replaces how a shape renders with Liquid that you write in the admin.

3
00:00:09,984 --> 00:00:15,500
In this video, you override a shape, find the names to override, and manage your templates.

4
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:22,682
Orchard Core builds every page from shapes.

5
00:00:22,682 --> 00:00:26,108
A shape has a type and a list of alternates, which are more

6
00:00:26,108 --> 00:00:27,583
specific names for it.

7
00:00:27,583 --> 00:00:30,640
The alternates are tried when the shape is rendered.

8
00:00:31,390 --> 00:00:38,518
A blog post shown in a list is a Content_Summary shape, and Content_Summary__BlogPost is its alternate.

9
00:00:38,518 --> 00:00:42,899
A view file of the theme answers to that name, and a template

10
00:00:42,899 --> 00:00:44,730
answers to it as well.

11
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:48,795
The alternates are tried from the most specific one.

12
00:00:48,795 --> 00:00:53,824
For each alternate, Orchard Core looks for a template first, then for a view file.

13
00:00:53,824 --> 00:00:57,310
So a template wins over a view file with the same name.

14
00:00:58,060 --> 00:01:00,811
Templates apply to the pages of the site.

15
00:01:00,811 --> 00:01:06,371
The admin screens use the Admin Templates feature instead, which a separate video covers.

16
00:01:06,371 --> 00:01:10,760
A template lives in the site's database, so saving it needs no build.

17
00:01:11,510 --> 00:01:14,132
Editing templates takes two permissions:

18
00:01:14,132 --> 00:01:17,096
Manage templates, and Manage Liquid templates.

19
00:01:17,096 --> 00:01:21,656
You need both to see the menu item, and both are marked security critical.

20
00:01:21,656 --> 00:01:25,190
The Administrator and Editor roles have them by default.

21
00:01:29,399 --> 00:01:32,905
On the blog page, every post is listed by a summary view that

22
00:01:32,905 --> 00:01:34,318
comes from the theme.

23
00:01:34,318 --> 00:01:36,359
Let's replace it with a template.

24
00:01:36,819 --> 00:01:42,186
Under Design, Templates, the list is empty, because the site has no templates.

25
00:01:42,186 --> 00:01:44,359
Add Template opens the form.

26
00:01:44,813 --> 00:01:47,816
The name is the alternate to override:

27
00:01:47,816 --> 00:01:50,000
Content_Summary__BlogPost.

28
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,913
The description is optional, and the list shows it under the name.

29
00:01:55,371 --> 00:01:56,861
Content is required.

30
00:01:56,861 --> 00:02:01,386
If you save without it, the form refuses, and suggests Placements in case

31
00:02:01,386 --> 00:02:03,391
you wanted to hide the shape.

32
00:02:03,845 --> 00:02:06,590
Content takes the Liquid, in a code editor.

33
00:02:06,590 --> 00:02:11,575
A template replaces the whole view, so it must write everything the shape displays.

34
00:02:12,026 --> 00:02:17,489
After Save and Cancel, the Preview button opens a page for previewing templates.

35
00:02:17,489 --> 00:02:19,586
A separate video covers it.

36
00:02:20,040 --> 00:02:23,735
Save stores the template and returns to the list, where it appears with

37
00:02:23,735 --> 00:02:25,510
its description under the name.

38
00:02:25,963 --> 00:02:30,208
Back on the blog page, every post is drawn by the template, without

39
00:02:30,208 --> 00:02:33,987
the date and the badges, which the template does not write.

40
00:02:33,987 --> 00:02:38,463
Nothing was built or restarted, and the theme's view file is unchanged.

41
00:02:42,663 --> 00:02:45,434
The documentation lists the names to use.

42
00:02:45,434 --> 00:02:50,031
Content items are named by their type and display type, widgets by their

43
00:02:50,031 --> 00:02:53,981
type, and parts, fields and zones have patterns of their own.

44
00:02:53,981 --> 00:02:57,163
A single item can be named by its slug or alias.

45
00:02:57,913 --> 00:03:01,569
In a template, Model is the shape being rendered.

46
00:03:01,569 --> 00:03:07,552
For a content item, Model.ContentItem is the item, with its DisplayText, its Author,

47
00:03:07,552 --> 00:03:11,473
and, under Content, the data of its parts and fields.

48
00:03:12,223 --> 00:03:16,505
Model.Content is a zone with the shapes of the parts and fields.

49
00:03:16,505 --> 00:03:22,621
The shape_render filter draws them, and the shape_remove_item tag takes one out by name first.

50
00:03:22,621 --> 00:03:25,923
A separate video covers Liquid shapes and zones.

51
00:03:26,673 --> 00:03:29,242
Other shapes have other properties.

52
00:03:29,242 --> 00:03:33,189
A widget has Classes, and a zone has ZoneName and Parent.

53
00:03:33,189 --> 00:03:38,263
The documentation lists the properties of content items, widgets and zones.

54
00:03:42,464 --> 00:03:46,916
To find the shape behind a fragment of a page, open Settings, Debugging,

55
00:03:46,916 --> 00:03:49,884
check Write shape debug information, and save.

56
00:03:51,484 --> 00:03:55,583
In the page source, each rendered shape is then wrapped in comments.

57
00:03:55,583 --> 00:03:59,958
The start comment gives the shape type, and the binding that rendered it:

58
00:03:59,958 --> 00:04:03,558
the alternate that matched, and where its markup came from.

59
00:04:03,558 --> 00:04:05,884
For a view file, it names that view.

60
00:04:06,634 --> 00:04:10,043
Choose the most specific name that does what you need.

61
00:04:10,043 --> 00:04:14,930
A template named for the shape type itself, such as Content_Summary, answers for

62
00:04:14,930 --> 00:04:19,134
every content type in summary views that has no more specific match.

63
00:04:23,349 --> 00:04:27,610
Content types have shortcuts, so you don't have to type names.

64
00:04:27,610 --> 00:04:33,500
Under Design, Content Definition, Content Types, edit Blog Post and open Edit templates.

65
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:37,699
It lists an entry for detail views and one for summary views.

66
00:04:38,153 --> 00:04:42,623
The detail entry stands for the alternate Content__BlogPost.

67
00:04:42,623 --> 00:04:47,703
No template has that name, so the form opens with the name filled in.

68
00:04:48,159 --> 00:04:49,707
Cancel leaves the form.

69
00:04:49,707 --> 00:04:54,351
The summary entry opens the template you created earlier, because it exists, with

70
00:04:54,351 --> 00:04:56,059
its content ready to edit.

71
00:05:00,272 --> 00:05:04,699
In the list, the search box filters templates by name, and Edit opens

72
00:05:04,699 --> 00:05:07,592
one with its name, description and content.

73
00:05:08,050 --> 00:05:09,773
The name is editable too.

74
00:05:09,773 --> 00:05:14,220
Saving under another name moves the template to another shape, and the old

75
00:05:14,220 --> 00:05:16,110
name goes back to the theme.

76
00:05:16,572 --> 00:05:22,092
Delete asks for confirmation in a dialog, and then removes the template from the site.

77
00:05:22,092 --> 00:05:23,532
The list is empty.

78
00:05:23,987 --> 00:05:28,124
The blog page shows the summary view of the theme, because no template

79
00:05:28,124 --> 00:05:29,867
answers to that alternate.

80
00:05:34,067 --> 00:05:38,890
Templates are data of the site, so recipes and deployment plans can carry them.

81
00:05:38,890 --> 00:05:44,337
The Templates recipe step defines each template by name, with its description and content.

82
00:05:45,087 --> 00:05:48,948
A deployment plan exports them with the All Templates step.

83
00:05:48,948 --> 00:05:53,225
Export templates as files writes each one to its own file, and the

84
00:05:53,225 --> 00:05:55,067
step refers to that file.

85
00:05:57,067 --> 00:05:58,744
That's the Templates module:

86
00:05:58,744 --> 00:06:02,297
Liquid stored under the name of a shape, written in the admin, and

87
00:06:02,297 --> 00:06:04,764
tried before a view file with the same name.

88
00:06:04,764 --> 00:06:06,047
Thanks for watching.

